# 4x DGX Spark profile (TP4/EP4). Copy to .env.tp4 and edit. Never commit .env.tp4.
# Used by ./start-tp4.sh; ./start.sh keeps using .env (3 Sparks).

# ─── cluster ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
HEAD_IP=10.0.0.1
WORKER_IPS="10.0.0.2 10.0.0.3 10.0.0.4"
WORKER_HOSTS="spark2 spark3 spark4"
WORKER_USER=zurih
# Different login names per worker: set WORKER_USER="", define User in each
# WORKER_HOSTS entry in ~/.ssh/config, and set an explicit absolute WORKER_DIR.
SSH_IDENTITY=$HOME/.ssh/id_ed25519_shared

# ─── fabric (ConnectX-7 RoCE triangle) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Gloo/NCCL TCP bootstrap on the LAN NIC. Data plane prefers RoCE (NCCL_NET=IB).
# The two *_SOCKET_IFNAME knobs below bind the bootstrap; FABRIC_IFACE is kept for
# reference only and nothing reads it.
# The socket interface names are host-specific and must exist under the SAME name on
# every node (`ip -br link` on each); a name missing on one node kills that rank in
# Gloo init ("Unable to find address for: ..."). This fleet uses enp1s0f0np0; enP7s7
# below is the upstream example's host.
FABRIC_IFACE=enp1s0f1np1
# Socket interface names are machine-specific; doctor checks them on every node.
# DOCTOR_STRICT=1 makes missing interfaces or inactive listed HCAs blocking.
GLOO_SOCKET_IFNAME=enP7s7
NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=enP7s7
# RDMA device names (not interfaces). The production line is measured with one port
# of each ConnectX-7 PCI domain; the switchless ring uses both ports of the first card
# instead (IB_HCA=rocep1s0f0,rocep1s0f1, see docs/switchless-ring.md).
# List only ports whose state is ACTIVE on every node, here and in B12X_ROCE_HCA:
#   for d in /sys/class/infiniband/*; do echo "$(basename $d) $(cat $d/ports/1/state)"; done
# Which port of a card is cabled differs between fleets. Read `state`, not `rate`: a
# DOWN port still reports 40 Gb/sec.
IB_HCA=rocep1s0f0,roceP2p1s0f0
NCCL_NET=IB
NCCL_IB_DISABLE=0
NCCL_P2P_DISABLE=1
NCCL_SHM_DISABLE=1
NCCL_DEBUG=WARN
# NCCL connection buffers live in pinned host RAM, which on GB10 is GPU memory:
# the defaults (4 MiB Simple + 4.69 MiB LL128 + 0.5 MiB LL per connection, 512
# connections) cost 4.7 GiB per node. Decode all-reduces are 61 KB (LL protocol),
# prefill uses Simple; LL128 buys nothing here. Measured: 5.0 GB -> 0.14 GB pinned.
NCCL_BUFFSIZE=1048576
NCCL_LL128_BUFFSIZE=262144
NCCL_PROTO=^LL128
NCCL_MAX_NCHANNELS=8
# Set NCCL_DEBUG=INFO for one boot to see communicator/channel/protocol choices.
NCCL_DEBUG_SUBSYS=INIT,ENV
NCCL_HOST_DIR=$HOME/nccl-2.30.7

# ─── Optional: switchless ring (no RoCE switch) ───────────────────────────────
# Every default above is for a switched fabric. If the four Sparks are cabled as a
# ring (a-b-c-d-a, one DAC per adjacency, no switch) the stack does NOT boot on
# those defaults: NCCL builds a tree as well as the ring, the tree wants a direct
# fabric path between opposite nodes (rank0 <-> rank2) which a four-node ring does
# not have, and RoCE queue pairs do not follow IP routing, so the tree never
# connects and `ncclCommInitRank` dies with "NCCL error: unhandled system error".
#
# Uncomment the three lines below to turn the ring on. Everything is opt-in and
# gated: with NCCL_SWITCHLESS_RING_ONLY unset this file behaves exactly as before.
# Full cabling/runbook: docs/switchless-ring.md
#
# Requires a PATCHED NCCL in NCCL_HOST_DIR (from FujitsuPolycom/sparkring, the
# `switchless-cycle` + `skip-tree-pat` patches). The preflight refuses a stock
# build by looking for the SWITCHLESS_RING_ONLY marker in the library.
#NCCL_SWITCHLESS_RING_ONLY=1
#NCCL_ALGO=Ring
#NCCL_P2P_LEVEL=SYS
# The overlay mount is what makes this compatible with DeepEP: mounting the patched
# library OVER the image's pip NCCL keeps one runtime visible, whereas
# LD_LIBRARY_PATH leaves two and DeepEP's check_nccl_so() aborts before NCCL starts.
# Defaults to following the switch above; set explicitly only to override.
#NCCL_OVERLAY_PIP=1
#NCCL_PIP_SO=/opt/sglang/lib/python3.12/site-packages/nvidia/nccl/lib/libnccl.so.2
# Rank 2 (opposite the head) has no direct fabric path to rank 0, so the ring must
# skip the tree connect and use subnet-aware routing. Both are set by files/nccl.sh
# when the switch is on; override here only if your IB_HCA naming needs it.
#NCCL_SKIP_TREE_CONNECT=1
#NCCL_IB_SUBNET_PREFIX_LEN=24
# ─── Optional: the second ConnectX-7 (two PCI root domains) ────────────────────
# NCCL accepts any number of devices in IB_HCA but publishes at most TWO listener
# GIDs, so every device past the second is invisible to peers and carries zero
# bytes -- with no error and a channel plan that still names it. `doctor` warns
# about that. To actually use all four, build NCCL with sparkring's cumulative
# nccl-2.30.7-dual-pci-domain patch (it already contains switchless-cycle; do not
# layer the two) and uncomment the block below. See docs/switchless-ring.md
# ("Devices past the second are never advertised").
# The flags are only half of it: the second card's two ports also need an IPv4,
# MTU 9000 and a populated GID at NCCL_IB_GID_INDEX, plus a route to the two /24s
# this node does not sit on. A cabled but unaddressed port publishes nothing and
# stays at zero bytes -- netplan template in the same document ("The second
# plane's network has to exist before those flags can reach it").
#NCCL_IB_EXTENDED_IPV4_GIDS=1
#NCCL_IB_PRESERVE_PCI_DOMAIN=1
#NCCL_IB_ROUTE_DIAGNOSTICS=1
#NCCL_IB_QPS_PER_CONNECTION=1
# Four devices, four channels: one channel per device is the mapping that reaches
# all of them. Eight still works (0,1,2,3,0,1,2,3) but measured no serving benefit
# on this workload while costing 0.14 GiB of head shared memory.
#NCCL_MAX_NCHANNELS=4
# List every device, not only the first two, and add NET while validating -- the
# ListenerRouting record is the only place that says whether the cap is still on:
#   NCCL INFO NET/IB ListenerRouting format=ipv4-v1 advertised=4 observed=4
#NCCL_DEBUG_SUBSYS=INIT,ENV,NET

# Weights: a ring has no fabric-wide NFS path, so each node keeps its own copy.
# Uncomment this together with the three ring lines above. `start.sh doctor`
# then checks every worker's local volume, and `serve` refuses to start four
# containers around a half-provisioned node. See docs/switchless-ring.md
# ("Local weights").
#NFS_SHARE=0
#
# Validate before serving: ./start.sh doctor prints one line per rank, e.g.
#   [+] switchless ring: config OK (NNODES=4 TP=4 EP=2, IB_HCA=rocep1s0f0,rocep1s0f1)
#   [+] switchless ring: head preflight OK (RoCEv2 GID index 3)
#   [+] switchless ring: 10.0.0.2 preflight OK (RoCEv2 GID index 3)
# A failure there is fatal in `serve`, before any container is replaced.
# Expected NCCL log lines on a successful boot:
#   NCCL INFO Connected all rings, use ring PXN 0 GDR 0
#   NCCL INFO NCCL_SWITCHLESS_RING_ONLY set by environment to 1.
#   NCCL INFO Tree transport setup disabled by NCCL_SWITCHLESS_RING_ONLY
#   NCCL INFO PAT transport setup disabled by NCCL_SWITCHLESS_RING_ONLY
#
# RoCEnante on the ring. Its one-shot all-reduce/all-gather writes into every peer, and a ring has no
# link between opposite nodes, so without more a ring runs the production line with
# SGLANG_ROCE_ALLREDUCE=0 and no DSV41_ROCE_GATHER. docs/switchless-ring.md ("RoCEnante on the ring")
# builds the opposite-node path in the neighbours' ConnectX-7 hardware (FujitsuPolycom/sparkring) and
# scripts/ring_mesh/plan.py prints the line for your fleet: the peer maps depend on the cabling and
# on the TP rank order. Append it to the production EXTRA_CONTAINER_ENV, replacing that line's
# B12X_ROCE_HCA, SGLANG_ROCE_MAX_SIZE and DSV41_ROCE_GATHER values:
#   DSV41_ROCE_RING=1 SGLANG_ROCE_ALLREDUCE=1 SGLANG_ROCE_MAX_SIZE=262144 DSV41_ROCE_GATHER=262144 B12X_ROCE_HCA=rocep1s0f0,rocep1s0f1,roceP2p1s0f0,roceP2p1s0f1 B12X_ROCE_PEER_HCA_MAPS=<plan.py> B12X_ROCE_TWO_WAVE_THRESHOLD_BYTES=0
# 262144 needs hairpin_queue_size 8192 on every fabric function (dsv41-mesh.service sets it at boot);
# at the driver default of 1024 use 81920.

# ─── model / image ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
MODEL_DIR=$HOME/NewModels/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017
COMMON_MODEL=/var/tmp/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017
# BASE_IMAGE is passed as the serving Dockerfiles' FROM build argument.
# Default: the dev-dsv41 multi-arch index this recipe was built and tested on, pinned by digest; the tag itself moves
# upstream and the runtime patches check exact files of this build (issue #78). Override only with a known-good base.
# Offline: docker load retains tags but not RepoDigests. SKIP_PULL=1 requires
# the base tag on every node and strips @sha256:... before inspect and FROM.
# It skips registry pulls only; canary wheel installation still needs a mirror.
#SKIP_PULL=1
BASE_IMAGE=lmsysorg/sglang:dev-dsv41@sha256:3dbc313030a6ef2c5d7de8ecf48e9aece722694a82182cb618cc82b588816349
IMAGE=dsv41-4x-spark:local
PORT=8888

# Weights: `./start-tp4.sh download` uses the `hf` CLI on the head and skips when the
# checkpoint is complete. EXPECTED_SHARDS is what doctor and download treat as
# "complete"; it has to match the revision (48 files for every checkpoint below).
# Pick one checkpoint profile; MODEL_DIR (above), HF_REPO and HF_REVISION go together, and
# the NFS_VOLUME / NFS_EXPORT_NAME names below should differ per checkpoint so a switch never
# mounts the other one. Both 1017 variants run the same image and serving profile:
#  - official DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017 (the default; the v2.6.2 release gate ran on it)
HF_REPO=deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017
HF_REVISION=1bae0e1088993b9b82de08f59de5be5a34e42170
#  - abliterated 1017, an optional alternative (refusals removed: rank-2 edit of attn.wo_b and
#    shared_experts.w2 in the 40 main layers; experts, Engram and the DSpark draft layers are
#    bit-identical). The DSpark draft was trained against the official weights; README lists the
#    speed of both.
#HF_REPO=mrfakename/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017-abliterated
#HF_REVISION=517efab9ccfef7f714dbd7d679f181a86f43ccf9
#  - the original V4.1-Flash release (before 1017)
#HF_REPO=deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash
#HF_REVISION=fb2764a5cf321eaa5070ca8f9e892818f477c16d
EXPECTED_SHARDS=48

# serve polls boot.py's ready banner this many times, 10 s apart (360 = one hour).
# Raise it if a first boot on cold caches takes longer.
READY_TIMEOUT=360

# ─── weights on spark2/spark3 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Always from the head over NFSv4 (no rsync, no SSHFS). With 4 nodes the fabric
# normally goes through a switch: give one server address for all workers, or one
# per worker in WORKER_IPS order (NFS_SERVER_IPS="a b c").
# spark2 mounts 10.0.22.1; spark3 mounts 10.0.23.1 (pairwise CX7).
NFS_SHARE=1
NFS_VOLUME=dsv41-weights-1017
NFS_EXPORT_NAME=dsv41-native-1017
NFS_SERVER_IPS="10.0.22.1"
NFS_CLIENTS=10.0.0.2,10.0.0.3,10.0.0.4,10.0.22.0/24

# ─── parallelism (4 Sparks) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TP=4 EP=4 matches world size. heads=64 / o_groups=8 / draft experts=128 /
# vocab=129280 all divide by 4, so no TP padding is needed (DSV41_TP_PAD=0 below).
NNODES=4
TP_SIZE=4
# 2 for the base and canary images (FlashInfer's MXFP4 MoE needs the per-rank expert width to be
# a multiple of 128). The production line below runs EP_SIZE=1 (every rank a 576-wide slice of all
# 384 experts, routed MoE on b12x main via DSV41_MOE_B12X_NEXT): no EP-group straggler at the MoE
# all-reduce. Set EP_SIZE=1 together with that line. EP_SIZE=4 does not boot with
# DSV41_MOE_B12X_NEXT (per-rank width 2304 misses both pinned plans, which need
# N % 128 == 64; "planned dynamic direct routing is unsupported for this launch shape").
EP_SIZE=2
DIST_PORT=20000

# ─── serving ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NVMe Engram offload is required on Spark (host RAM == GPU unified memory).
OFFLOAD_MODE=nvme
# Per-host cache budget, split across the 2 Engram layers and the ranks sharing
# that host. 0 disables it: with the packed local shards a miss is ONE local
# read, and the measured miss rate costs ~145 reads/s against a drive that does
# ~112k IOPS. Host RAM is GPU memory here and rank 0 has ~0.3 GB available, so
# the GiB this used to hold is worth far more as headroom than as hit rate.
# The published results are measured at 4. On GB10 the cache comes out of the KV
# pool's headroom: one ring fleet reported +9 % KV pool and +1.5 GiB MemAvailable at 0
# (MiaAI-Lab/DeepSeek-v4.1-Flash-DGX-Sparks#41); not re-measured here.
DSV41_CACHE_GIB=4
# GB10 NVMe: ~3.5k IOPS at QD=1, ~112k at QD=64. The Engram callback blocks the
# compute stream, so misses are serviced by a pool rather than one at a time.
DSV41_IO_THREADS=96
# The drive's power saving (APST) puts it to sleep after ~100 ms without commands
# and the next read pays ~10 ms to wake it; a c16 step is ~145 ms, so every step's
# Engram reads paid it. row_store reads one 4 KiB page every 25 ms while lookups
# arrived in the last 2 s (on by default; 0 disables, max 10000):
# DSV41_ENGRAM_KEEPALIVE_MS=25
# Pinning the owned scale rows makes a miss cost one pread instead of two
# (~1.8x on the callback), but it needs ~1 GiB/layer/rank of EAGER host RAM, and
# host RAM here is GPU memory: with weights at ~104 GB the pin raised SGLang's
# "minimum viable" mem-fraction from ~0.940 to 0.9569 and left no KV cache.
# Off until the packed local shard lands, which gets the same single read from
# disk layout instead of RAM.
DSV41_RESIDENT_SCALES=0
DSV41_CACHE_WAYS=16
DSV41_STATS_SECONDS=60
CONTEXT_LENGTH=1048576
# 0.80 of each rank's 121.7 GiB is the static pool (weights + KV + CUDA graphs);
# the other ~24 GiB stays outside it for the prefill transient (the indexer's
# per-chunk buffers, see CHUNKED_PREFILL_SIZE) and runtime slack. The KV budget
# SGLang reports on the boot line (full_token=) shrinks with this fraction, so
# MAX_TOTAL_TOKENS below is sized for the 0.80 budget, not the old 0.90 one.
MEM_FRACTION_STATIC=0.80
# Rank 0 pays ~1.3 GiB the workers do not (HTTP server + tokenizer +
# detokenizer) and also serves the checkpoint over NFS. The knob exists to tune
# it separately, but note LOWERING it does not give the head headroom: weights
# cost the same either way, so a smaller fraction only starves the KV pool.
# Measured on this box: 0.9436 is where KV goes negative, and 0.945 still leaves
# only 0.12 GB -- less than the 0.34 GB DSV4 SWA cap. 0.95 is the working value.
# Those are TP3 measurements; at TP4 the head simply tracks MEM_FRACTION_STATIC.
HEAD_MEM_FRACTION_STATIC=0.80
# 8 concurrent requests. This also sets --cuda-graph-max-bs-decode (start.sh
# passes the same value through), so CUDA-graph capture covers batch tiers 1-8.
MAX_RUNNING_REQUESTS=16
# 1024-token prefill chunks. The indexer's per-chunk transient peaks at about
# 14 B x chunk x prefix (measured, docs/chunked-prefill-memory.md): ~15 GB for a
# 1024-token chunk at 1M context, ~60 GB for 4096, which no rank has. Verified:
# a 1M-context needle test passed on TP4 at 1024 (the 3-Spark fleet reached
# 208k single / 186k concurrent at the same chunk). Raise it for faster
# short-prompt prefill only after a 1M prompt has passed
# scripts/verify/memguard.py at the larger size.
CHUNKED_PREFILL_SIZE=4096
# Pin the KV pool. Unpinned, SGLang sizes it from whatever is free at load time,
# which on a unified-memory box is the OS MemAvailable: it swings with the page
# cache and makes the pool (and decode speed) a boot lottery. Pinned, SGLang
# demands exactly this, and a pin that does not fit either dies ("no room ...
# within the available X GB") or is silently clamped down -- both have happened
# on this stack -- so the pin has to sit under the smallest budget a boot will
# ever compute, not at the ceiling.
# Read the budget off the boot log:
#   DSV4 memory calculation: bytes_per_full_token=1670.75 ... full_token=<N>
# At TP4 with MEM_FRACTION_STATIC=0.90 that line reported ~16M tokens (~26.7 GB
# per rank). This profile runs 0.80, which takes ~13 GB off that budget, and
# pins 8,000,000 x 1,670.75 B = 13.4 GB of KV per rank under it. Tested: the
# profile boots and serves at these values and a 1M-context needle test passed.
# If the pool ever has to shrink, lower this pin rather than raising the memory
# fraction: the fraction is what keeps the prefill transient safe. With
# MAX_RUNNING_REQUESTS=8 the pool holds 8 requests averaging 1,000,000 tokens
# each (8 x 1Mi = 8,388,608 would be the pin for all 8 at the full context).
# Pages are touched only when used, so the pool costs nothing until it fills.
MAX_TOTAL_TOKENS=8000000
SPEC_ALGO=DSPARK
DSPARK_BLOCK_SIZE=5
# Profile with sglang.benchmark.dspark_sps_profiler into state/dspark_sps.json;
# until that file exists DSpark runs the verify-all schedule and gains nothing.
# V4.1 Flash's Engram path is incompatible with the compact ragged verify that
# this table enables. boot.py therefore ignores even an existing table unless
# DSPARK_SPS_ALLOW_ENGRAM=1 explicitly acknowledges a future engine fix (put it in
# EXTRA_CONTAINER_ENV: a plain .env line does not reach the containers).
DSPARK_SPS_TABLE=/state/dspark_sps.json
DSPARK_STS_TABLE=/state/dspark_sts.json

# ─── engram local shards ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ./start.sh pack repacks each rank's owned Engram rows (weight+scale adjacent)
# onto node-local NVMe: ~48 GiB/node at TP4 (~63 GiB at TP3), one read per miss
# instead of two, and no NFS hop on the workers. Serving picks them up
# automatically when present; the shard names carry the TP size.
ENGRAM_DIR=$HOME/dsv41-engram
DSV41_PACKED_DIR=/engram
SKIP_PREPARE=1
SKIP_VERIFY=1
SKIP_SMOKE=0
SMOKE_QUICK=1

# ─── kernels (GB10 / SM121) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The 32x32 ue8m0 block-FP8 dense projections only have a Triton kernel unless a
# FlashInfer backend is named, which routes them to the MXFP8 dense kernels.
# --watchdog-timeout covers the CuTe-DSL JIT compile of the b12x kernels on the
# first forward after a boot.
# --min-free-slots-delay 1 disables the admission delayer, which is what makes
# MAX_RUNNING_REQUESTS=8 actually reachable. SGLang treats DSPARK as
# DFlash-family (spec_info.py: is_dflash_family = is_dflash or is_dspark), and
# for that family with max-running-requests >= 8 it auto-enables a threshold of
#   min(4, max(2, (max_running_requests + 5) // 6))
# (srt/managers/min_free_slots_delayer.py), i.e. 2 at 8 requests and 3 at 16.
# New prefills are then held until that many slots are free, so with steady
# arrivals the batch never sustains the configured max -- ~7 of 8, ~13-14 of 16.
# An explicit value wins and <= 1 disables it. Externally reported: +43% c8
# aggregate on a 3-Spark fleet. Not yet measured here. Drop this flag to get the
# batched admissions back if per-admission cost (DSpark's draft prefill pass)
# ever dominates at high concurrency.
# --sleep-on-idle: the scheduler stops busy-polling when there is nothing to do. Measured
# 2026-09-18: head scheduler idle CPU 47 % -> 14 %, workers 5 %; first response after 20 s
# of idle 0.18-0.21 s either way; decode unchanged. Suggested by RustyAiLab (Mia #16).
EXTRA_SGLANG_ARGS="--fp8-gemm-backend flashinfer_cutlass --watchdog-timeout 1800 --min-free-slots-delay 1 --enable-deepseek-v4-fp4-indexer --enable-cache-report --sleep-on-idle"
# With DSV41_FAST_LOAD=1 (below) the loader's shard window is what bounds the host
# memory the eager reads hold: num_threads + 1 shards, ~3.4 GB each on TP4/EP2.
# Append to the line above:
#   --model-loader-extra-config {\"num_threads\":1}
# Engine metrics are opt-in. Without both flags, /metrics returns 404 even
# though host-level monitoring still works. Append when engine telemetry is used:
#   --enable-metrics --enable-metrics-for-all-schedulers
# The exported names are sglang:* rather than vllm:*.
# Which FlashInfer MXFP8 kernel serves those projections (adapter/mxfp8_b12x.py):
# b12x is FlashInfer's own SM120/121 choice (16|32-row tiles, ~3x the CUTLASS
# SM120 kernel at decode M); cudnn is the alternative; cutlass restores SGLang's
# default, which measured no faster than Triton (both ~50 ms of a decode step).
DSV41_MXFP8_BACKEND=b12x
# adapter/shared_pad_k.py: at TP4 the shared expert's down_proj is N=5120 K=576, and
# 576 is not a multiple of 128, so b12x rejects it and this ONE shape in the model
# falls back to a CUTLASS kernel that pads M to 128 and runs at 25% occupancy
# (52 GB/s against the 196 GB/s b12x reaches on the same data). Padding K to 640 with
# zero weights puts it back on b12x: measured bit-identical (0 of 30720 elements
# differ), costs 14 MB per rank, and removes 0.90 ms from a 61.3 ms decode step.
# Padding alone buys nothing -- CUTLASS at K=640 measured 0.7% SLOWER than at 576 --
# so this is worth nothing on a stack where b12x is not the MXFP8 backend.
DSV41_SHARED_PAD_K=1
# FlashInfer's fused MoE finalize sums the top-k expert outputs with atomic bf16
# adds. The autotuner picks it for the 32-token bucket (17-32 token prefills,
# decode at concurrency 3-4), which made greedy outputs nondeterministic by up to
# ~1 nat on first-token logprobs. Off = separate finalize kernel, ~0.3 ms/step.
SGLANG_FLASHINFER_MOE_FUSED_FINALIZE=0

# Reasoning budget for thinking-mode requests that carry no reasoning_effort.
# SGLang's V4.1 encoder maps low/high/xhigh/max to 25/50/75/100 and defaults to
# "high" = 50; the publisher's encoding/encoding.py says low=50, high=75, max=100
# with default 75. adapter/sitecustomize.py remaps the named tiers to the
# publisher's table; this pins the default as a number so it holds even without
# the remap. Integer 1-100 or low/high/xhigh/max. Per request: OpenAI
# reasoning_effort (a tier, or a 0-0.99 float = budget/100) or
# chat_template_kwargs {"reasoning_effort": N}. Only rendered in thinking mode.
SGLANG_DSV41_REASONING_EFFORT=75

# When a chat request omits max_tokens / max_completion_tokens, fill this in and
# also clamp larger client values. 32768 would have stopped the 714k-token
# racing-animation loop while covering the visual/HTML trials (~15k). 0 restores
# SGLang's fill-the-remaining-context behaviour (1M on TP4). Raise it for long
# thinking-on traces.
DSV41_MAX_NEW_TOKENS=32768

# Decode-side loop abort. --watchdog-timeout only fires when a step hangs; a live
# token stream (VIS-04: 714k tokens of racing-animation HTML) is healthy as far
# as the GPU watchdog is concerned. End the request after four copies of a
# ≥32-token n-gram, 64 identical tokens, or eight identical decoded lines.
# The 2× EXL3 recipe has no equivalent. 0 disables.
DSV41_LOOP_ABORT=1
DSV41_LOOP_NGRAM=32
DSV41_LOOP_REPEATS=4
DSV41_LOOP_LINE_REPEATS=8
# How a loop-aborted request ends. 1: as an error whose message starts with
# "loop_abort:" (streaming: an error event; non-streaming: HTTP 500), so agent
# clients see a failed step instead of a finished turn. 0: the old exit,
# finish_reason=stop / matched_stop=repetition, which looks like a normal end
# (an agent stalls on a reasoning-only step, a tool call is cut mid-arguments).
DSV41_LOOP_NAMED_EXIT=1
# A request whose history holds an assistant tool call with arguments that are
# not a JSON object (e.g. one cut by the loop abort) is rendered with
# {"_unparseable_arguments": <raw text>} instead of failing with HTTP 400.
# 0 restores the 400.
DSV41_HISTORY_REPAIR=1

# PyTorch caching allocator mode. The DSV4 prefill allocates a larger indexer-logits
# buffer for every 2048-token chunk (each chunk scores the whole prefix so far); the
# native allocator cannot reuse smaller freed blocks for the next larger request and
# reserved memory grows with the SQUARE of the prompt length (measured: 8 GiB reserved,
# 0 live, after 64 growing steps), which is the 64k-128k prefill OOM on the head.
# expandable_segments coalesces freed space: 0.6 GiB after 128 steps in the same test.
# NOT expandable_segments:True: on the 3-node GB10 fleet it made every prefill of more
# than 64 query tokens return NaN logits (REPORT.md section 17). Untested at TP4.
PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:False

# adapter/prefill_empty_cache.py (in the shared image): after every prefill chunk of a
# sequence at least this long, return PyTorch's unused cached blocks so a long prompt
# costs one chunk's transient instead of the sum over chunks (REPORT.md section 17b).
# The per-chunk indexer buffer is CHUNKED_PREFILL_SIZE x prefix x 4 B per ratio-1 source
# layer, ~14 B x chunk x prefix at the peak across layers: at 1M context a 1024-token
# chunk peaks around 15 GB, against the ~24 GiB per rank that MEM_FRACTION_STATIC=0.80
# leaves outside the static pool (verified: 1M-context needle test passed on TP4). Use
# scripts/verify/memguard.py when ramping new prompt shapes, and drop the chunk to 512
# if the guard fires. 0 disables the hook.
DSV41_PREFILL_EMPTY_CACHE_TOKENS=8192

# adapter/swa_cone.py (DSV41_SWA_CONE_REPLAY; module off when unset): when a cached prefix lost the SWA
# window at its resume point F but its full KV is resident, the request replays only DSV41_SWA_CONE_TOKENS
# (default 8192 = one chunk of CHUNKED_PREFILL_SIZE 8192) rows before F instead of re-prefilling from the
# last surviving window (often token 0: 55-130 s at 300-700K tokens). Same exactness class as a prefix hit;
# see the module. Each replay logs one "DSV41_SWA_CONE plan" and one "copy" line. Never set
# DSV41_SWA_CONE_TEST in serving (test-only forced eviction). In the production profile since v2.6.4
# (DSV41_SWA_CONE_REPLAY=1 DSV41_SWA_CONE_TOKENS=8192): replays measured at 1.6-2.1 s instead of 22-68 s
# re-prefills at 130-400K tokens.
# adapter/swa_thin.py (DSV41_SWA_THIN; module off when unset): a cached prefix is reusable only where
# its last 128 tokens still hold SWA slots, and the SWA pool is small (cap mode: request cap 35,328 +
# --swa-prefix-tails x 384 slots). Every 8192-token prefill chunk leaves a 512-slot checkpoint and
# every agent turn ~512 more, so in stock LRU order an idle session loses its resume point after
# ~70-100K slots of other traffic and its next turn re-prefills the whole context although the full
# KV is still cached (2026-11-04 production: 8 re-prefills of 170-680K tokens in 6 h, 32-129 s
# each). 1 = a checkpoint its own request has moved past is evicted first: chunk boundaries a chunked
# prefill has passed, and the prefix-match node of a request that needed no chunked step (an agent
# turn's previous resume point). `chunk` = the chunk boundaries only. Only the SWA LRU order changes:
# kernels, pool sizes and outputs are untouched. Append to EXTRA_CONTAINER_ENV (a plain .env line
# does not reach the containers).
# --swa-prefix-tails sizes the pool. Each slot costs ~23.9 KB per rank (swa_fixed 1.88 GiB at
# 84,480 slots); the SWA pool is a fixed cost subtracted from the KV budget before the full pool is
# sized, so 384 tails (182,784 slots, +2.2 GiB) cost ~1.4M full-pool tokens (measured 5.06-5.16M ->
# 3.60-3.75M) and change nothing else: the static pool and the head's MemAvailable are the same. The
# pin below (8,000,000) stays above the profiled value either way. In the production profile since
# v2.6.4: DSV41_SWA_THIN=1 with --swa-prefix-tails 384 (was 128).
# adapter/swa_prompt_tail.py (DSV41_SWA_PROMPT_TAIL_KEEP=<tokens>, 1..8192; module off when unset; in the production
# profile since v2.7 at 1024): from Eric Jinho Jang (dealign.ai, jjang-ai/dealignai-deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017-4x-dgx-spark c175335 /
# a9f87fe; their value 1024). The prompt-end insert keeps only one page (256 tokens) of live SWA below
# page_floor(prompt), so a revisit whose common prefix ends earlier (a chat turn with a new question, a document with
# another suffix) re-prefills from 0 on its 2nd visit and hits from the 3rd (theirs: 6k chat turn 2 0 % -> 93.7 %
# cached). With N set the prompt-end insert keeps N live SWA tokens there. Decode frees stay stock once the tree owns
# the prompt end (theirs held every decode slot until the request finished); a prompt end still request-owned in
# decode keeps its tail for at most DSV41_SWA_PROMPT_TAIL_HOLD (8192) more tokens. Cost: each cached prompt end holds
# up to N + 256 SWA slots instead of 256. Only when request-owned SWA is freed changes: outputs are untouched.

# ─── TP4 runtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Memory per rank at TP4: ~77 GiB of MXFP4 experts + FP8 dense weights and a
# ~2.5 GiB draft, so ~40 GB of the 121.7 GiB stays free (vs ~6 GB at TP3). That
# pays for the settings above: the full 1M context, an 8M-token KV pool
# (1,670.75 B/token/rank = 13.4 GB), 8 concurrent requests, 1024-token prefill
# chunks (~15 GB peak indexer transient at 1M), and a 0.80 memory fraction that
# keeps ~24 GiB per rank outside the static pool for that transient. Packed Engram
# shards are named engram-l<layer>-r<rank>of4.bin and can share ENGRAM_DIR with a
# 3-Spark profile. At TP4 every shard holds real weights, so no head padding.
DSV41_TP_PAD=0
WORKER_DIR=/home/$WORKER_USER/dsv41-4x-spark

# ─── Extra container env (mirrored to every rank by start.sh) ───────────────
# DSV41_INDEXER_CHUNKED=1 arms the sglang#39187 backport (adapter/indexer_chunked*.py):
# the dense prefill indexer scores each request in row chunks of at most
# DSV41_INDEXER_LOGITS_BUDGET_BYTES (default 2 GiB) of fp32 logits and publishes only
# the tail rows of the candidate masks. That bounds the per-chunk transient that made
# CHUNKED_PREFILL_SIZE=1024 necessary (docs/chunked-prefill-memory.md), so the 4096
# chunk above is safe at any context: measured head MemAvailable low-water 7.0-7.7 GiB
# during a 262k cold prefill on both images (docs/window-20260916.md).
# adapter/fast_load.py (docs/fast-load.md): this rank's checkpoint tensors are read
# eagerly by a 16-thread pread pool into anonymous host memory instead of being
# page-faulted through the loader's mmap at 0.5 GB/s, copies are paced to
# DSV41_FAST_LOAD_INFLIGHT_GB so the reads stay just ahead of them, and the DSpark
# draft load opens only the 3 shards that hold mtp.* tensors. load_weight 285 s -> 77 s,
# engine start 356 s -> 125 s, values bitwise identical (tests/test_fast_load_pacing.py,
# and the in-image checkpoint test described in the docs). EP_SIZE / N_EXPERTS are
# fallbacks for when the parallel context is not readable from the loader.
# The eager tensors are views into 256 MiB pinned slabs (DSV41_FAST_LOAD_SLAB_MB, 0 = one
# pinned buffer per tensor); DSV41_FAST_LOAD_MEMLOG=1 logs host counters and pinned
# allocation counts at the end of each load (diagnostics only).
#   DSV41_FAST_LOAD=1 DSV41_FAST_LOAD_EP_SIZE=2 DSV41_FAST_LOAD_N_EXPERTS=384 DSV41_FAST_LOAD_INFLIGHT_GB=6
# adapter/engram_prefetch.py: the Engram row lookups (NVMe misses inside a host callback,
# 2.2-2.4 ms of GPU idle per step on real text) run on a side stream right after the
# hash ids exist and join only at the layer's gather. Same-image A/B 2026-09-18:
# step 51.7 -> 49.5 ms, English essay c1 42.6 -> 45.1 tok/s, sparkDash prose c1 57-59 ->
# 60-61, prose c4 120 -> 124, structured 116 -> 121; bitwise identical rows
# (DSV41_ENGRAM_PREFETCH_CHECK=1 re-runs the old path and counts differences: 0).
#   DSV41_ENGRAM_PREFETCH=1
# adapter/wo_a_w8.py: the verify/draft wo_a projection (2-8 rows) reads the checkpoint's own
# e4m3 bytes plus one exponent per 32x32 block instead of the bf16 copy the engine makes at load,
# in a copy of the stock Triton kernel that rebuilds the same bf16 tile in registers. A layer is
# converted only when its fp8 twin reproduces the bf16 weight exactly (43 of 43 here). 43 layers
# at M=6: 3.43 -> 2.20 ms; decode step at c1 52.9 -> 51.7 ms; the MXFP8-epilogue path is bitwise
# identical, the plain bf16 path differs in fp32 accumulation order only.
#   DSV41_WO_A_W8=1
# adapter/draft_tau.py: draft proposal temperature for sampled requests. Proposal and acceptance
# use the same tempered q, so the output distribution is unchanged (speculative sampling is exact
# for any q); at the model card's T=1 / top_p=0.95, 0.8 raises accepted tokens per step by ~1.2 %
# (offline, held-out traffic). Needs SGLANG_DSPARK_FOLDED_SAMPLING=2.
#   DSV41_DRAFT_TAU=0.7
# adapter/draft_head_fp8.py: the draft reads an fp8 copy of the (shared) LM head; the target's
# logits are untouched, so outputs cannot change. 1405 -> 721 us per step, acceptance unchanged.
#   DSV41_DRAFT_HEAD_FP8=1
# adapter/block_verify.py: block verification (Sun et al., ICLR 2025) instead of token-by-token
# rejection for sampled rows; exact output distribution (chi-square test in tests/), more tokens
# accepted per step: +1.8 % on prose, +2.5 % on coding-with-thinking (engine-exact p and q).
#   DSV41_BLOCK_VERIFY=1
# adapter/block_verify_fold.py (v2.6.3): the same block verification for sampled rows in 3 Triton
# kernels plus the torch path's own three reductions, instead of ~55 eager torch launches; tau and the
# correction token are bit-identical to DSV41_BLOCK_VERIFY alone for the same random draws (=check runs
# both and counts mismatches on device). Sampled c1 step -0.5 %; greedy requests never take this path.
#   DSV41_BLOCK_VERIFY_FOLD=1
# adapter/folded_result_fence.py: folded all-greedy verify results are cloned off the verify
# graph's persistent buffers before the overlapped copy to host (sglang#40919 race).
#   DSV41_FOLDED_FENCE=1
# adapter/verify_cap.py: per-request verify length. conf:T verifies only the leading drafts whose
# running product of the draft confidence head's survival stays >= T; the other verify rows reuse
# the anchor row's experts (no extra expert reads, ~2 ms per row at c1) and acceptance is capped at
# the verified drafts. Exact (greedy identical, sampled via block_verify). conf:0.1 measured best:
# prose c1 +5 %, sampled thinking +5 %, code flat. 1..5 = fixed verified drafts (measurement only).
#   DSV41_VERIFY_CAP=conf:0.1
# adapter/autotune_keep.py: keep the FlashInfer autotune cache across boots under EP (sglang#40320).
#   DSV41_AUTOTUNE_KEEP=1
# adapter/wo_a_w8.py, two more switches (need DSV41_WO_A_W8=1): MID runs verify/draft wo_a at 9-192
# rows (c2..c32) from the fp8 twin too; DROP then releases the bf16 copy (722 MB per rank -> KV pool).
#   DSV41_WO_A_W8_MID=1 DSV41_WO_A_W8_DROP=1
# adapter/wo_a_input_fp8.py (OFF by default, in no profile line): round wo_a's input o to e4m3 in 32-value
# blocks with power-of-two scales and back, as DeepSeek's reference inference code does since 2026-10-12
# (target and DSpark draft attention). Same-image A/B on the production profile, 2026-10-22: sparkDash
# prose / code c1 85.4 / 140.3 vs 88.6 / 144.9 off, speedbench all 85.1 vs 85.9; qeval 71/75 and T>0 scan
# 0/35 either way. Numerics parity at a ~3 % decode cost. Set it identically on every rank.
#   DSV41_WO_A_INPUT_FP8=1
# adapter/row_store.cpp: keep one Engram layer's row cache in the GB10 display reservation (outside
# MemAvailable; host needs nvidia_drm modeset=1 fbdev=0 and no desktop, see docs/display-reserve.md).
#   DSV41_ENGRAM_DRM_NODE=/dev/dri/card0
# adapter/replicated_split.py: ReplicatedLinear layers whose prefix ends with one of the listed
# suffixes are column-split over TP (each rank runs the same quantized linear on its 128-row weight
# tiles, then an all-gather). Per layer and only if the slice is bit-identical to the stock layer on
# every rank (checked at boot). engram.wkv (183 MB read by every rank at both Engram layers) and
# wqkv_a: c1 step -1.4 ms, prose c1 +3.7 %, greedy identical.
#   DSV41_REPLICATED_SPLIT=wqkv_a,engram.wkv
# adapter/router_live.py (needs DSV41_VERIFY_CAP): the router kernel loads the anchor row's scores for
# dead verify rows, so they get the anchor's experts without the separate remap launch (40 per step);
# built from the engine's own router source, bit-identical outputs.
#   DSV41_ROUTER_LIVE=1
# adapter/draft_main_proj.py: the draft's replicated main_proj (105 MB fp8 on every rank) as a
# 1/TP column shard (fp8, exact weight bytes) + all-gather; draft only, target untouched.
#   DSV41_DRAFT_MAIN_PROJ_SPLIT=1
# adapter/roce_gather.py (canary-roce image): TP all-gathers up to N bytes per rank (the draft's
# vocab-parallel logits, the splits above) take the RoCEnante one-shot kernel instead of NCCL.
#   DSV41_ROCE_GATHER=2097152
# adapter/l2_prefetch.py (canary-roce image): during each RoCE collective of a decode step a side
# stream prefetches the first 6 MB of the weights that follow into L2 (plans learned from the warm-up
# forwards, captured in the graphs). WOA adds a window after wq_b that prefetches wo_a while the
# attention core runs: -0.4 ms/step. Its other sub-gates measured flat or slower; leave them unset.
#   DSV41_L2_PREFETCH=1 DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_WOA=1
# adapter/replicated_split.py, compact gather: each rank sends only its own columns and RoCEnante
# writes them in place (no pad / reorder); checked byte for byte against the old path per layer and
# row count at boot. Part of the 2026-09-25 bit-exact bundle with the wqkv_a window GEMM (default on)
# and the b12x_next barrier / planner patches: -0.4 ms/step prose, -0.45 ms code.
#   DSV41_SPLIT_COMPACT_GATHER=1
# adapter/spec_sync_free.py: drops the per-step rank-0 broadcasts whose values are already identical
# on every rank (draft token, accept results, verify_cap length); the draft noise is a counter-based
# stream shared by all ranks. audit mode counted 0 mismatches in ~60k checks; -0.2..-0.55 ms/step.
#   DSV41_SPEC_SYNC_FREE=all
# adapter/eager_glue.py: fewer eager kernels between the draft and verify graphs (one fused fence
# copy, cached sampling-param staging, verify_cap's update inside the draft graph as one kernel);
# bit-identical. all = fence,stage,vcap,vcapk (needs DSV41_FOLDED_FENCE / DSV41_VERIFY_CAP for
# fence / vcap).
#   DSV41_EAGER_GLUE=all
# adapter/cert_head.py: certified target LM head for greedy verify steps. An 8-bit (MXINT8) screen of
# the head shard with a proven error bound picks the tiles that can hold the argmax; only those are
# computed with the exact kernel (bit-identical to the stock matmul), the rest are -inf, so the greedy
# accept is unchanged. Sampled rows, logprobs, grammar and penalties get the full exact logits. M lists
# the verify row counts qualified by tests/gpu_test_cert_ds.py (bs 1..8; slower from bs 10 up).
# c1 step -0.5 ms, sparkDash prose c1 +1.2..+1.9 %, +173 MB per rank.
#   DSV41_CERT_HEAD=1 DSV41_CERT_HEAD_M=6,12,18,24,30,36,42,48
# Not in the production line: DSV41_AB_VARIANTS (in-boot A/B harness, test only, adapter/ab_variant.py).
# DSV41_PREFILL_SP_FP8_MOE=1 (MXFP8 MoE-input gathers in prefill SP) is in the production line since v2.7.3.
EXTRA_CONTAINER_ENV="DSV41_INDEXER_CHUNKED=1"
# SGLang #41259/#45410 parallelizes compatible long rendered prompts by
# default. Its compatibility checks fall back to tokenizer.encode. To force a
# rollback, append SGLANG_PARALLEL_PROMPT_ENCODE=0 to EXTRA_CONTAINER_ENV on
# every rank; the default threshold is 32768 characters.

# The dsv4.1 branch (canary images) probes a Rust toolchain to build its image
# preprocessor and can hang in `cargo --version` before the HTTP server starts
# (seen 2026-09-17: launch_server stuck 20+ min, workers up, /health 000).
# `never` skips Cargo and keeps the PIL path. Harmless on the base image.
# Reported on 2026-09-16 branch images: the probe still ran and hung ~4.5 min despite
# `never` (MiaAI-Lab/DeepSeek-v4.1-Flash-DGX-Sparks#45). If the boot log shows
# "V4.1 Rust image processor unavailable; using PIL" after a long pause, adding
# RUSTUP_DIST_SERVER=http://127.0.0.1:1 RUSTUP_UPDATE_ROOT=http://127.0.0.1:1
# CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true to EXTRA_CONTAINER_ENV makes the probe fail at once.
SGLANG_RUST_BUILD_MODE=never

# ─── Optional: upstream dsv4.1 branch image (Dockerfile.canary) ──────────────
# Build with scripts/fetch-sglang-canary.sh + `docker build -f Dockerfile.canary
# -t dsv41-4x-spark:canary .` on every node, or let ./start-tp4.sh build do it on
# every node with BUILD_DOCKERFILE below (#BUILD_ARGS passes extra docker build
# flags, e.g. --build-arg PIP_INDEX=<mirror>/simple without pypi.org). Then point
# IMAGE at it and force the graph-folded draft sampler (greedy-folded by default):
#IMAGE=dsv41-4x-spark:canary
#BUILD_DOCKERFILE=Dockerfile.canary
#EXTRA_CONTAINER_ENV="DSV41_INDEXER_CHUNKED=1 SGLANG_DSPARK_FOLDED_SAMPLING=2 SGLANG_RUST_BUILD_MODE=never"

# ─── Optional: canary + RoCEnante (Dockerfile.canary-roce) ─────────────────────
# Routes TP SUM all-reduces up to SGLANG_ROCE_MAX_SIZE bytes (2 MiB covers the
# 16-request decode step) to the b12x one-shot RDMA all-reduce over both rails.
# DSV41_ROCE_TWOSHOT=1 DSV41_ROCE_TWOSHOT_ROWS=<rows> sends all-reduces of at least <rows> rows
# through the pipelined two-shot RoCE all-reduce (flashinfer#6915 port, bit-identical to the
# one-shot); B12X_ROCE_PIPE_BLOCKS / B12X_ROCE_PIPE_CHUNK_BYTES (defaults 4 / 131072) tune it.
# The production profile below uses 18 rows, 4 blocks and 65536-byte chunks. Not with
# DSV41_ROCE_RING=1.
# DSV41_ROCE_IDLE_PARK=1 (off by default, needs DSV41_ROCE_TWOSHOT=1): the RDMA proxy threads
# block on a futex while the scheduler is idle instead of nanosleep-polling, woken before every
# forward. DSV41_ROCE_IDLE_PARK_MS=1000 (idle time before parking), DSV41_ROCE_IDLE_PARK_POLL_MS=20
# (backstop re-check while parked). In the production profile since v2.6.3: idle proxy CPU 6x lower,
# speed and greedy outputs unchanged, first token after 60 s idle +3 ms.
# DSV41_TOKENIZER_HEAP=trim2 (off by default; in the production profile since v2.6.5; adapter/heap_trim2.py): launch_server
# (the head's tokenizer process) gives its glibc heap back to the OS. Long prompts arriving several at a time grow
# that heap by GiBs of free but resident memory (1.9 -> 5-7 GiB in a 15-min long-prompt soak on v2.6.4). malloc_trim(0)
# after >= DSV41_TOKENIZER_TRIM2_MIN_MIB (256) of new heap, at most once per DSV41_TOKENIZER_TRIM2_MIN_INTERVAL_S
# (120) s, when no prompt was rendered / encoded for DSV41_TOKENIZER_TRIM2_IDLE_S (1.5) s or, under a stream of
# back-to-back prompts, at the end of a prompt's tokenization DSV41_TOKENIZER_TRIM2_MAX_BUSY_S (30) s later. Allocation
# only: outputs unchanged. Receipts: armed / hooks / per-trim / summary lines in the head log.
# DSV41_SWA_NATIVE_DECODE=1 (canary images; off by default): the decode attention (<= 64 query rows: the
# DSpark verify and draft forwards) reads the SWA pool in its own 256-token pages instead of copying every
# touched page into four 64-token pages first (mask fill + page mark + page split, 43 times per c1 step).
# Same FlashInfer kernel body at 256-token pages, same tactic, same output bits; unserved shapes keep the
# split. Module built at image build (runtime/flashinfer_v41c, .so sha256 checked at load). In the
# production profile since v2.6.4: greedy c1 +1.4 / +1.9 % in two in-boot A/Bs on 4 Sparks.
#IMAGE=dsv41-4x-spark:canary-roce
#EXTRA_CONTAINER_ENV="DSV41_INDEXER_CHUNKED=1 SGLANG_DSPARK_FOLDED_SAMPLING=2 SGLANG_ROCE_ALLREDUCE=1 SGLANG_ROCE_MAX_SIZE=2097152 B12X_ROCE_HCA=rocep1s0f0,roceP2p1s0f0 B12X_ROCE_CACHE_DIR=/state/b12x-roce B12X_COMPILE_CACHE_DIR=/state/b12x-compile SGLANG_RUST_BUILD_MODE=never"
# Prefill TP split (SG18) on top of either canary image, from 32k context:
#EXTRA_CONTAINER_ENV="DSV41_INDEXER_CHUNKED=1 SGLANG_DSPARK_FOLDED_SAMPLING=2 SGLANG_ROCE_ALLREDUCE=1 SGLANG_ROCE_MAX_SIZE=2097152 B12X_ROCE_HCA=rocep1s0f0,roceP2p1s0f0 B12X_ROCE_CACHE_DIR=/state/b12x-roce B12X_COMPILE_CACHE_DIR=/state/b12x-compile SPARK_PREFILL_TP_SPLIT=1 SPARK_PREFILL_TP_MIN_CONTEXT=32768 SPARK_PREFILL_TP_MIN_ROWS=1024 SGLANG_RUST_BUILD_MODE=never"
# Conservative fallback profile: the v2.1 feature set, plus the SGLang #41303 safety
# backport baked by scripts/fetch-sglang-canary.sh, without any v2.2-v2.4-only adapter or
# config delta. ~5 % slower than the production profile below (sparkDash prose c1 84.8 vs
# 89.1 on the 1017 checkpoint, 2026-10-22); keep it for diagnosis.
#EXTRA_CONTAINER_ENV="DSV41_INDEXER_CHUNKED=1 SGLANG_DSPARK_FOLDED_SAMPLING=2 SGLANG_ROCE_ALLREDUCE=1 SGLANG_ROCE_MAX_SIZE=2097152 B12X_ROCE_HCA=rocep1s0f0,roceP2p1s0f0 B12X_ROCE_CACHE_DIR=/state/b12x-roce B12X_COMPILE_CACHE_DIR=/state/b12x-compile SPARK_PREFILL_TP_SPLIT=1 SPARK_PREFILL_TP_MIN_CONTEXT=32768 SPARK_PREFILL_TP_MIN_ROWS=1024 SGLANG_RUST_BUILD_MODE=never DSV41_FAST_LOAD=1 DSV41_FAST_LOAD_EP_SIZE=1 DSV41_FAST_LOAD_TP_SLICE=auto DSV41_FAST_LOAD_N_EXPERTS=384 DSV41_FAST_LOAD_INFLIGHT_GB=6 DSV41_ENGRAM_PREFETCH=1 DSV41_SHARED_PAD_K=1 DSV41_WO_A_W8=1 DSV41_DRAFT_TAU=0.7 DSV41_DRAFT_HEAD_FP8=1 DSV41_BLOCK_VERIFY=1 DSV41_FOLDED_FENCE=1 DSV41_VERIFY_CAP=conf:0.1 DSV41_AUTOTUNE_KEEP=1 DSV41_WO_A_W8_MID=1 DSV41_WO_A_W8_DROP=1 DSV41_REPLICATED_SPLIT=wqkv_a,engram.wkv DSV41_DRAFT_MAIN_PROJ_SPLIT=1 DSV41_ROCE_GATHER=2097152 DSV41_ROUTER_LIVE=1 DSV41_MOE_B12X_NEXT=1 DSV41_MOE_B12X_NEXT_DETERMINISTIC=1 DSV41_HC_FUSED=1 DSV41_PREFILL_SP=1 DSV41_PREFILL_SP_FP8=1 DSV41_L2_PREFETCH=1"
#CHUNKED_PREFILL_SIZE=4096
#MEM_FRACTION_STATIC=0.80
#HEAD_MEM_FRACTION_STATIC=0.80
#EXTRA_SGLANG_ARGS="--fp8-gemm-backend flashinfer_cutlass --watchdog-timeout 1800 --min-free-slots-delay 1 --enable-deepseek-v4-fp4-indexer --enable-cache-report --sleep-on-idle"

# Production profile (v2.7.1, the README's current results; canary-roce image, EP_SIZE=1 above):
# the v2.4 lever set on an image with the issue #16 backport (#41303). Re-qualified
# 2026-10-22 on the 1017 checkpoint: the accelerated issue #16 reproducer
# (scripts/issue16_repro.py, 190K-900K cached prefixes + 512-4096 fresh tokens, c1/c4)
# answered 1,445 requests with 0 failures and no CUDA error or Xid on any rank.
# v2.6 (2026-10-27) adds the two-shot all-reduce for steps of >= 18 rows
# (DSV41_ROCE_TWOSHOT=1 DSV41_ROCE_TWOSHOT_ROWS=18 B12X_ROCE_PIPE_BLOCKS=4
# B12X_ROCE_PIPE_CHUNK_BYTES=65536: c16 +4-5 %) and two prefill host-overhead cuts
# (DSV41_MOE_LAUNCH_PREPARED=plan DSV41_INDEXER_NOSYNC=1: cold 4k prefill +16 %, TTFT -4 %);
# T=0 outputs byte-identical. Unset those six to return to v2.5.
# v2.6.2 (2026-10-28) adds DSV41_PREFILL_NATIVE_EXTRA=1: prefill reads the extra cache's native
# 128/256-token pages instead of a persistent 64-token copy (-2.5 to -2.8 GiB per rank allocated
# after pool sizing, cold 1M prefill -5 %); T=0 outputs byte-identical. Unset it to return to v2.6.
# v2.6.2 also adds DSV41_PREFILL_BYPASS_SOLO=1 (with DSV41_PREFILL_BYPASS=256): a short request that
# arrives during a long prefill runs in a pass of its own (first token 0.42 s instead of 1.9 s at
# 93K-128K; the long prompt's chunks and outputs are those of an idle run). Unset it to return to v2.6.
# v2.6.3 (2026-11-02) adds DSV41_BLOCK_VERIFY_FOLD=1 (sampled verify in 3 kernels, bit-identical to the
# torch path) and DSV41_ROCE_IDLE_PARK=1 (RDMA proxy threads park on a futex while the engine is idle).
# Unset both to return to v2.6.2. Its canary-roce image also applies FlashInfer PR #7676's proxy fence to
# the b12x dense MXFP8 GEMM at build time (a race fix, no switch; each rank logs "DSV41 b12x dense GEMM
# fence (FlashInfer #7676): present" at boot).
# v2.6.4 (2026-11-05) adds DSV41_SWA_NATIVE_DECODE=1 (decode reads the SWA pool's own 256-token
# pages, no per-call 64-token split; bit-identical). Unset it to return to v2.6.3.
# v2.6.4 also adds DSV41_DECODE_YIELD=33 (running streams keep ~1/3 of their decode rate while
# another prompt prefills in chunks; that prompt's first token ~1.5x later). Unset it to
# return to the stock prefill / decode cadence (--prefill-decode-interval 2 only).
# v2.6.4 also adds DSV41_SWA_THIN=1 with --swa-prefix-tails 384 (was 128) in EXTRA_SGLANG_ARGS below:
# idle agent sessions keep their prefix-cache resume points (resumed 300-330K sessions 61-64 s -> 3.9-6.5 s);
# the full KV pool shrinks from ~5.1M to ~3.4-3.7M tokens (the SWA pool grows by 98,304 slots). And
# DSV41_SWA_CONE_REPLAY=1 DSV41_SWA_CONE_TOKENS=8192: a resume point that is evicted anyway costs a
# one-chunk replay (~2 s) instead of a whole-context re-prefill. Rollback, config only: unset
# DSV41_SWA_CONE_REPLAY / DSV41_SWA_CONE_TOKENS and DSV41_SWA_THIN, and set --swa-prefix-tails 128
# (back to the ~5.1M pool); tails 256 is the middle step if in-flight context ever needs > 3.4M tokens.
# v2.6.5 adds DSV41_KV_LAYOUT=v41c DSV41_V41C_ARM=1 DSV41_V41C_READER=native ("v41n"): the ratio-1/2
# compressed KV records are stored as V4.1 FP4 (288 B instead of 584 B) and read in place, so the same KV bytes hold
# x1.83 the tokens (912.25 instead of 1670.75 B per full token). Outputs stay in the V4 exactness class (the readers
# rebuild the V4 operand bytes); c16 costs ~0.4-0.8 %, c1 / c4 / prose ~0. And DSV41_TOKENIZER_HEAP=trim2: the head's
# tokenizer process gives its free glibc heap back (it grew by GiBs under back-to-back long prompts); allocation only.
# MAX_TOTAL_TOKENS=8541952 at fraction 0.85 below pins the v41n pool (see the comment there). Rollback, config only:
# unset the three v41n keys, set MAX_TOTAL_TOKENS=8000000 and the fraction 0.78 (v2.6.4: ~3.4-3.7M tokens); unset
# DSV41_TOKENIZER_HEAP on its own.
# v2.7 adds DSV41_SWA_PROMPT_TAIL_KEEP=1024 (adapter/swa_prompt_tail.py, hold DSV41_SWA_PROMPT_TAIL_HOLD at its default
# 8192): a prompt end keeps 1024 tokens of live SWA, so the next visit of a document or chat with a new question reuses
# the shared prefix on its 2nd visit instead of its 3rd (4 Sparks, 11-07: 2k / 6k revisits 97.0 / 99.0 % cached where
# v2.6.5 cached 0, a 6k chat turn 2 97.3 %). Each cached prompt end holds up to 1280 SWA slots instead of 256; outputs and
# the pool are unchanged. Rollback, config only: unset it. Its canary-roce image also carries NCCL 2.30.7 rebuilt with
# the NVIDIA/nccl #2393 AArch64 CTS fence (no switch; see Dockerfile.canary-roce). (These were the v2.6.6 candidate,
# not released on its own.)
# v2.7: with DSV41_KV_LAYOUT=v41 boot.py never lets FlashInfer calibrate sparse MLA in-process (a calibration after the
# CUDA graph capture faulted on GB10): --flashinfer-autotune-skip-ops sparse_mla_sm120 + the shipped calibration in
# /opt/dsv41/files/fi-autotune (FLASHINFER_AUTOTUNE_DIR); DSV41_V41_CALIBRATION=inprocess is for diagnostics only.
# v2.7 (FlashInfer 0.7.2) replaces the three v41n keys, DSV41_SWA_NATIVE_DECODE=1 and DSV41_PREFILL_NATIVE_EXTRA=1 with
# DSV41_KV_LAYOUT=v41 SGLANG_SM120_V41_PRECISION=bf16: the SWA (main) cache and the draft's paged SWA hold KVLayout.V41
# (528 B: all 512 values e4m3 in groups of 32 with ue8m0 scales, RoPE included, as DeepSeek's V4.1 reference stores the
# window K), the ratio-1/2 caches V41_FP4 (288 B, as v41n), read in place by FlashInfer's SM120 DSv4.1 kernels on the
# full-BF16 route (bf16 Q and P, exact on-chip dequantization). A numerics change against v2.6.5 (RoPE in fp8 in the SWA
# cache). v2.7 release gate (2026-11-08, against two v2.6.5 boots): KLD 0.011 short / 0.006 long (limit 0.035), qeval
# 71 / 75 unchanged, draft acceptance x1.006-1.046, decode at parity, cold prefill 64K-~400K ~1.7 % slower, needles
# 256K / 1M found (details in CHANGELOG.md). FlashInfer 0.6.18-only knobs refuse at boot. Rollback: the v2.6.5 image
# and profile.
# v2.7.1 adds five lossless changes (T=0 outputs unchanged by construction; each measured on 4 Sparks 2026-11-08/09
# against production v2.7 in the same boot, official 1017 checkpoint):
# DSV41_PREFILL_GEMM_ROW_SPLIT=4096 DSV41_PREFILL_GEMM_ROW_SPLIT_N=1792,1152 (adapter/prefill_gemm_split.py): at an
# 8192-row prefill chunk the two K = 5120 MXFP8 GEMMs (wqkv_a, shared-expert FC1) run as two 4096-row launches of the same
# b12x kernel, so the 21 MB activation block stays in L2 instead of being re-read from DRAM per N tile; the first call of
# every shape runs the stock GEMM and the split on the same input and the ranks agree on a bit-for-bit match, else that
# shape keeps the stock call (prefill +3.8 % at 131K, +3.1 % at 32K, +3.5 % at 1M).
# DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_WOA_MB=0 DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_WOB_MB=6 (adapter/l2_prefetch.py, no new code): the attention-core L2
# window prefetches 6 MB of wo_b instead of wo_a (wo_b was the one main-stream dense weight still ~60 % cold; decode c1
# +1.1 %, 6 of 6 rounds).
# Stage 1 of diagnostics/megakernel-20261109 (both off by default, in-boot A/B keys; in the production line since the
# v2.7.3 candidate, see below): DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_WOB2_MB (adapter/l2_prefetch.py v6): a second wo_b L2 window forked at
# the wo_a call, taking the wo_b bytes the attention-core window left (meant with DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_WOA_MB=6
# DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_WOB_MB=0, i.e. wo_a warm in the attention core and wo_b warmed during wo_a's own run);
# DSV41_WO_A_W8_EVICT_FIRST=1 (adapter/wo_a_w8.py): wo_a's single-use twin stream carries an L2 evict-first policy so the
# prefetched wo_b lines outlive it. Cache hints only.
# DSV41_EXTEND_NOSYNC=1 (adapter/extend_nosync.py) with DSV41_MOE_LAUNCH_PREPARED=plan,direct (was plan): every extend
# builds its late-layer tail metadata and the Engram hasher's extend map without host syncs, and eager MoE launches skip
# the PyTorch dispatcher (c16 per-stream decode +3.4 %, c4 +3.0 %, c1 first token -41 ms).
# DSV41_SAMPLED_COMMIT_REUSE=1 DSV41_SAMPLED_DRAFT_PROBS_EARLY=1 (adapter/sampled_commit_reuse.py,
# adapter/sampled_draft_probs_early.py): sampled (T > 0) steps reuse the verify graph's draft-KV commit projection and
# compute the draft probabilities while the verify graph runs (T 1.0 prose c1 step -0.25 ms); greedy steps untouched.
# DSV41_PREFILL_BYPASS=8192 (was 256; adapter/prefill_bypass.py, no code change): a request with up to 8192 fresh tokens
# that arrives during a long prefill is admitted beside it (its own solo pass, as before) instead of only <= 256 tokens:
# a 500-token prompt beside a cold 131K prefill answers in 1.3-1.7 s instead of 26-28 s; the long prompt takes 6-10 %
# longer while such requests arrive. In production since 2026-11-08.
# Rollback, config only: unset the seven new keys, set DSV41_MOE_LAUNCH_PREPARED=plan and DSV41_PREFILL_BYPASS=256
# (= the v2.7 profile).
# Shipped with v2.7.1, default off and not recommended yet: DSV41_PREFILL_KEEP_CACHE=1 (adapter/prefill_keep_cache.py;
# not re-validated after its 11-09 fix) and DSV41_CERT_HEAD_MASK_MAX_M=30 (adapter/cert_head.py; not measured alone).
# v2.7.2 adds one lossless change: DSV41_PREFILL_CED_SKIP=1 (adapter/prefill_ced_skip.py; a port of Christopher Owen's
# spark-ds41f keep-one-decoder-row skip, vLLM patch 0255). Under the decoder SWA bounded replay every prefill chunk runs
# layers 20-39 over its last 128 rows; for a request with at least 128 prompt rows still to come after the chunk, that
# tail becomes the chunk's last row. Nothing reads the skipped rows (the next chunk's tail and decode stay inside their
# own window, a prefix hit re-prefills one window, the cone replay floors at its start). Measured on 4 Sparks
# 2026-11-09 against v2.7.1 in the same boot (official 1017): cold prefill +6.2 % at 131K, +3.7 % at 32K, +5.8 to +7.4 %
# at 1M; qeval 75 / 75 T = 0 outputs equal to the controls'; 32K first token bit-identical. Short prompts (one chunk)
# are not affected. Rollback, config only: unset it (= the v2.7.1 profile).
# v2.7.3 adds two lossless prefill changes, both bit-identical by a check on every rank (the ranks agree, MIN over TP):
# DSV41_PREFILL_SP_FP8_MOE=1 (adapter/prefill_sp.py stage 2b, in the image since 2026-09, off until now): at prefill SP
# chunks the MoE input is gathered as the MXFP8 bytes and scales b12x would compute itself (half the bf16 gather bytes;
# router and top-k on each rank's shard) and handed to b12x pre-quantized; the first chunk of every row count runs both
# paths and keeps the MXFP8 gather only if routing, the bytes and the MoE output are bit-identical on every rank.
# DSV41_PREFILL_GEMM_TACTIC=1 (adapter/prefill_gemm_tactic.py): eight measured prefill dense GEMM shapes (wo_b, q_b,
# indexer q_b at 8192 and 4096 rows, wqkv_a and shared FC1 at 4096) run FlashInfer's CUTLASS SM120 MXFP8 GEMM, tactic 5
# (CTA 128x128x128, swap_ab), instead of the b12x call; a boot check runs every shape both ways on three synthetic
# operand sets and keeps a shape only if the bytes are equal on every rank. Measured on 4 Sparks 2026-11-10 in the same
# boot as their controls (official 1017): stage 2b cold prefill +2.5 % at 131K (A/A 0.12 %); the tactic on top of it
# +1.9 % at 131K and +1.8 % at 32K (A/A 0.04 %); first tokens and continuations inside the controls' own states.
# Decode, verify and one-chunk prompts are not affected by these two.
# v2.7.3 also moves the decode attention-core L2 windows (no arithmetic change; cache hints only, outputs bit-identical):
# DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_WOA_MB=6 DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_WOB_MB=0 (was 0 / 6) DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_WOB2_MB=10
# DSV41_WO_A_W8_EVICT_FIRST=1: the window after wq_b warms 6 MB of wo_a again, and a second window forked at the wo_a call
# warms 10 MB of wo_b during wo_a's own run. Measured on 4 Sparks 2026-11-10 in one boot against the v2.7.2 profile
# (official 1017; tier-8 window M, arm X_w2): c1 greedy step -0.205 ms (paired per prompt, CI95 -0.30..-0.11), c1 sampled
# -0.30 ms, c4 +1.1 %, wo_a 44.6 -> 34.7 us per call (wo_b 23.9 -> 25.2 us: this layout drops the 6 MB wo_b window);
# qeval 75 / 75 T = 0 outputs equal to the controls'. Evict-first alone was flat (kept as measured).
# Rollback, config only: unset DSV41_PREFILL_SP_FP8_MOE, DSV41_PREFILL_GEMM_TACTIC, DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_WOB2_MB and
# DSV41_WO_A_W8_EVICT_FIRST and set DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_WOA_MB=0 DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_WOB_MB=6 (= the v2.7.2 profile).
# v2.7.4 adds lossless changes, each bit-identical by a check on every rank (the ranks agree, MIN over TP):
# DSV41_DENSE_TILE_TABLE=1 (adapter/dense_tile_table.py, hooked from adapter/mxfp8_b12x.py): three narrow decode MXFP8
# GEMMs (qa_kv 512x5120, shared FC1 1152x5120, draft wkv 1536x5120 per rank) at M <= 16 / <= 32 run the same b12x kernel
# on the (16, 64) / (32, 64) tile instead of the default plan's (64, 64); a single-GB10 sweep measured every tile config
# bit-identical and ~2x faster per call (warm L2). The first eager call of every (shape, M) runs both and keeps the tile
# only if the bytes are equal on every rank; a default-plan drift keeps production's tile. Measured on 4 Sparks
# 2026-11-10 in one boot against the v2.7.3 profile (official 1017; tier-10 window S2, arm X_d1, 8 rounds): c1 greedy
# step -0.132 ms (paired per prompt, CI95 -0.265..-0.0003); qeval 75 / 75 T = 0 outputs equal to the controls'. Prefill
# rows (M > 32) and every other shape are not affected.
# DSV41_PREFILL_MOE_FOLD=1 (adapter/prefill_moe_fold.py, kernel side scripts/b12x_next-topk-sum-accumulate.patch): a
# prefill MoE call (eager, >= 2048 rows) adds b12x_next's routed top-k sum straight into the shared experts' output inside
# the top-k sum kernel (out = bf16(float(out) + float(bf16(sum))), torch's add_ bit for bit) instead of a separate add
# (-168 MB of DRAM traffic and one launch per 8192-row layer). The first call of every plan capacity runs both ways and
# keeps the fold only if the bytes are equal on every rank. Measured on 4 Sparks 2026-11-11 in one boot against the
# v2.7.3 profile (official 1017; tier-13 window P, arm X_fold): cold 131K TTFT -0.96 % (~+1.0 % prefill; servebench
# 131K +0.8..+1.1 % in 3 of 4 rounds), memory and decode flat; boot check bit-identical at both capacities on all ranks.
# Decode, verify and one-chunk prompts are not affected.
# DSV41_WO_A_CFG_TABLE=1 (adapter/wo_a_cfg_table.py, hooked from adapter/wo_a_w8.py): wo_a's 8-bit twin GEMM at M = 6
# (c1; the partial route) launches with 2 warps, 2 stages and one exp2 per 32x32 scale block (production's tile, grid,
# BK, SPLIT_K and k order) and at M = 96 (c16; the mid route) with 2 warps instead of 4 (production's split_k / BM): a
# single-GB10 sweep measured both bit-identical to production's launch (warm 28.1 -> 11.8 us and -19.2 us per call).
# A load self-check on every rank (both launches on real bytes, a negative control that must differ, MIN over TP) and
# a byte check at the first eager call of every key keep production's launch on any difference; every other M is
# production's launch. Measured on 4 Sparks 2026-11-11 in one boot against the v2.7.3 profile (official 1017; tier-15
# window w, 6 rounds): c1 greedy step -0.140 ms (paired per prompt, CI95 -0.283..+0.003), c4 / c16 step time -0.21 /
# -0.51 %; with DSV41_DENSE_TILE_TABLE together c1 -0.129 ms, c4 -0.82 %; qeval 75 / 75 T = 0 outputs equal to the
# controls'. Release gate (2026-11-11, v2.7.3 / v2.7.4 / v2.7.3, all three keys): cold prefill +1.8 / +2.2 / +1.0 %
# at 64K / 131K / 1M, decode c1 x1.002, c4 x1.010, c8 x0.990, c16 x1.000 (see CHANGELOG.md).
# Rollback, config only: unset DSV41_DENSE_TILE_TABLE, DSV41_PREFILL_MOE_FOLD and DSV41_WO_A_CFG_TABLE (= the v2.7.3 profile).
# v2.7.5: DSV41_MOE_SCALE_CODES=1 DSV41_MOE_SCALE_CODES_MIN_ROWS=2 (adapter/scale_codes.py, retain mode): the routed
# experts' UE8M0 scales as 4-bit codes beside the native scales (+2.03 GiB per rank), run only inside the decode /
# verify / draft CUDA graphs of >= 2 rows (the M=1 draft graphs and every eager forward stay native); bit-identical
# outputs. In-boot A/B against v2.7.4 (tier-12o window Q, official 1017): c16 -3.56 ms/step [-5.67, -1.44], c1 -0.32 ms,
# qeval 75 / 75 = A, draft acceptance bit-identical. Release gate (2026-11-12, v2.7.4 / v2.7.5 / v2.7.4, all five keys and
# the verify threshold): decode c1 x1.018, c4 x1.034, c8 x1.027, c16 x1.006, c1 step -0.54 ms paired; head low-water
# 6.19 GiB against 8.48 / 8.36 GiB (the codes' 2.03 GiB per rank); see CHANGELOG.md.
# v2.7.5: DSV41_MOE_EVICT_FIRST=1 DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_SHPF_MB=6 DSV41_L2_PREFETCH_SHPF_EL=0 (adapter/moe_b12x_next.py +
# scripts/b12x_next-evict-first.patch; adapter/l2_prefetch.py v5): the routed-expert weight copies of the decode / verify /
# draft graphs (<= 128 rows) carry an L2 evict_first policy (Christopher Owen's spark-ds41f #966, ported to b12x_next), so
# they stop flushing the shared-expert FC1 remainder + FC2 that a 6 MB window prefetches at the FFN-side hc entry (plain
# prefetch). Bit-identical by construction. In-boot A/B against v2.7.4 (tier-12o window P, arm X_ef2, official 1017):
# c1 paired step -0.19 ms [-0.34, -0.04], c4 -0.38 ms/step [-0.74, -0.01], qeval 75 / 75 = A. Release gate: with the
# scale codes above (see there and CHANGELOG.md).
# Rollback, config only: unset the five v2.7.5 keys (= the v2.7.4 profile; the image is the same).
# v2.7.5 (tier-16 lever X_vbs): DSV41_VERIFY_CAP=conf:0.1/4:0.25/8:0.1 (adapter/vcap_spec.py): the draft-confidence stop
# threshold is 0.25 at batch sizes 4-7 and stays 0.1 at 1-3 and >= 8 (a stopping rule: never changes which token is
